## emersion/go-imap v2  (wire layer)

The `imap` module is a PORT of `github.com/emersion/go-imap` v2, not a
clean-room implementation from RFC 9051.

⛔ CORRECTED 2026-09-09. This inventory said "Ported so far:" and then listed
ONE file, `utf7.zig`. Seven files carry a `Ported from emersion/go-imap` line in
their own headers — that is most of the module, and the inventory was wrong by
six of them. The reproduced MIT text below was complete the whole time, so
nothing was ever legally owed that was not paid; what was wrong was the record
of WHAT was taken, and a record that is wrong is worse than one that is absent,
because it answers the reader's question with a falsehood. The list below is now
derived from the tree (`rg -ln 'Ported from .*emersion/go-imap' modules/imap/src/`),
not maintained by hand.

Ported (7 files, verified against the tree 2026-09-09):

  * `utf7.zig` — `internal/utf7/{utf7,decoder,encoder}.go`: the modified-UTF-7
    codec, including its rejection rules (padding, null shift, non-canonical
    base64 runs, unpaired surrogates). The vector table in this module's tests
    is `internal/utf7/decoder_test.go`.
  * `wire.zig` — `internal/imapwire/decoder.go`: the IMAP wire grammar on the
    decode side (atoms, quoted strings, literals, `NIL`, numbers,
    parenthesised lists), including its tolerances for what real servers send.
  * `command.zig` — `internal/imapwire/encoder.go` plus the command builders in
    `imapclient`: the encode side, including when an `astring` must go as a
    literal rather than quoted.
  * `response.zig` — `imapclient/client.go`'s `readResponse` family: the
    three response shapes and the same real-server tolerances.
  * `client.zig` — `imapclient`: the session layer, reshaped from upstream's
    goroutine-per-connection model into a synchronous one.
  * `fetch.zig` — `imapclient/fetch.go`: `FETCH` and the `msg-att` reply,
    including the recursive `BODYSTRUCTURE` grammar.
  * `search.zig` — `imapclient/search.go`: `SEARCH` criteria and both the
    IMAP4rev1 and `ESEARCH` reply shapes.

